<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Servers often expect a value in a format that is awkward for users to type: a telephone area code
in parentheses, an amount with its currency code, a country calling code with a leading
<code>+</code>. Today authors either make the user type the extra characters, which invites mistakes
and needs looser validation, or add script that rewrites the value before the form is submitted.

The <{input/formatter}> attribute lets authors declare the submitted format instead. The user types
only the part that varies, and the user agent adds the rest when the form is submitted.

<div class=example id=example-intro-area-code>
    <xmp highlight=html>
    <form action="/register">
      <label>Area code
        <input type="tel" name="areaCode" maxlength="3" pattern="[0-9]{3}"
               formatter="({value})">
      </label>
      <button>Register</button>
    </form>
    </xmp>

    The user types <kbd>206</kbd>. The field shows <code>206</code>, the <{input/pattern}> attribute
    checks <code>206</code>, and {{HTMLInputElement/value}} returns <code>"206"</code>. Submitting the
    form sends:

    <pre>areaCode=(206)</pre>

    If the user leaves the field blank, the form sends <code>areaCode=</code>, not
    <code>areaCode=()</code>.
</div>

User agents that don't support this specification ignore the attribute and submit the plain value,
<code>areaCode=206</code>.


<h2 id=the-formatter-attribute>The <code>formatter</code> attribute</h2>

<h3 id=formattable-elements>Formattable elements</h3>

A <dfn export>formattable element</dfn> is either:

* an <{input}> element whose <{input/type}> attribute is in a state whose [=value mode=] is
    [=value mode/value=]; or
* a <{select}> element.

Note: The states whose [=value mode=] is [=value mode/value=] are Text, Search, Telephone, URL,
Email, Password, Date, Month, Week, Time, Local Date and Time, Number, Range, and Color. Checkboxes,
radio buttons, file uploads, hidden inputs, and buttons submit values that the author already
controls with the <{input/value}> attribute, so they have no need for a formatter.

<h3 id=the-content-attribute>The content attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for="input, select">formatter</dfn> content attribute is a template for the
value that the control submits. It can be specified on <{input}> and <{select}> elements. It must
only be specified on [=formattable elements=]. On any other <{input}> element, the attribute does
not apply and has no effect.

The template is made up of literal text and the value placeholder,
<code>{value}</code>, which stands for the control's value. The value placeholder can appear any
number of times, including not at all. To include a literal U+007B ({) or U+007D (}) in the
submitted value, authors write it twice: <code>&#123;&#123;</code> or <code>&#125;&#125;</code>.

<div class=example id=example-escaping>
    With a control whose value is <code>206</code>:

    <table class=data>
        <thead>
            <tr><th><{input/formatter}><th>Submitted value
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>({value})</code><td><code>(206)</code>
            <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;{value}&#125;&#125;</code><td><code>{206}</code>
            <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;value&#125;&#125;</code><td><code>{value}</code>
            <tr><td><code>{value}-{value}</code><td><code>206-206</code>
    </table>
</div>

If present, the attribute's value must be a <dfn export>valid formatter template</dfn>: a string
that, when [=parse a formatter template|parsed=], never reaches an [=unmatched brace=], and in which
every placeholder is <code>{value}</code>.

<div algorithm="parse a formatter template">
    To <dfn export>parse a formatter template</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|, run these steps. They
    return a [=list=] whose [=list/items=] are either [=strings=], which are literal text, or the
    special value <dfn>placeholder</dfn>.

    1. Let |tokens| be an empty [=list=].

    1. Let |literal| be the empty string.

    1. Let |position| be a [=string/position variable=] for |input|, initially pointing at the start
        of |input|.

    1. [=iteration/While=] |position| is not past the end of |input|:

        1. Let |c| be the [=code point=] at |position| within |input|.

        1. If |c| is U+007B ({) or U+007D (}), and the code point after |position| is also |c|,
            then append |c| to |literal|, advance |position| by 2, and [=iteration/continue=].

        1. If |c| is U+007B ({), then:

            1. Let |start| be |position|.

            1. Advance |position| by 1.

            1. Let |identifier| be the result of [=collecting a sequence of code points=] that are
                neither U+007B ({) nor U+007D (}) from |input|, given |position|.

            1. If |identifier| is not the empty string, and |position| is not past the end of
                |input|, and the code point at |position| is U+007D (}), then:

                1. If |literal| is not the empty string, then [=list/append=] |literal| to
                    |tokens|, and set |literal| to the empty string.

                1. If |identifier| is "<code>value</code>", then [=list/append=] [=placeholder=]
                    to |tokens|. Otherwise, the placeholder is replaced with nothing.

                1. Advance |position| by 1.

                1. [=iteration/Continue=].

            1. <dfn>Unmatched brace</dfn>: Set |position| to |start|.

        1. Append |c| to |literal|, and advance |position| by 1. If |c| is U+007B ({) or U+007D (}),
            this is also an [=unmatched brace=].

    1. If |literal| is not the empty string, then [=list/append=] |literal| to |tokens|.

    1. Return |tokens|.
</div>

Note: User agents are lenient with invalid templates. A brace that is neither doubled nor part of a
placeholder is kept as literal text, and a placeholder other than <code>{value}</code>, such as
<code>{Value}</code>, is replaced with nothing.

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn export>format a value</dfn> given a [=formattable element=] |element| and a [=string=]
    |value|:

    1. If |element| does not have a <{input/formatter}> attribute, or |value| is the empty string,
        then return |value|.

    1. Let |result| be the empty string.

    1. [=list/For each=] |token| of the result of [=parse a formatter template|parsing=] the value of
        |element|'s <{input/formatter}> attribute:

        1. If |token| is [=placeholder=], then append |value| to |result|.

        1. Otherwise, append |token| to |result|.

    1. Return |result|.
</div>

Note: Empty values are never formatted, so an optional field that the user leaves blank doesn't
submit the template's literal text.

<h3 id=the-idl-attribute>The IDL attribute</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
interface mixin FormatterAttribute {
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute DOMString formatter;
};
HTMLInputElement includes FormatterAttribute;
HTMLSelectElement includes FormatterAttribute;
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=FormatterAttribute>formatter</dfn> IDL attribute must [=reflect=] the
<{input/formatter}> content attribute.

<div class=example id=example-idl>
    <xmp highlight=js>
    const areaCode = form.elements.areaCode;

    areaCode.value;                         // "206"
    areaCode.formatter;                     // "({value})"
    new FormData(form).get("areaCode");     // "(206)"

    // Feature detection.
    const supported = "formatter" in HTMLInputElement.prototype;
    </xmp>
</div>


<h2 id=form-submission>Form submission</h2>

<h3 id=patch-construct-the-entry-list>Changes to constructing the entry list</h3>

The algorithm for [=constructing the entry list=] [[!HTML]] is changed as follows. Inserted text is
<ins>marked like this</ins> and removed text is <del>marked like this</del>. Steps that don't
change are summarized in brackets.

<div algorithm="construct the entry list (patched)">
    5. For each element |field| in <var ignore>controls</var>, in [=tree order=]:

        1. [The steps up to and including "Let <var ignore>name</var> be the value of the |field|
            element's <{input/name}> attribute" are unchanged.]

        1. If the |field| element is a <{select}> element, then for each <{option}> element in the
            <{select}> element's [=list of options=] whose [=selectedness=] is true and that is not
            [=option/disabled=], [=entry list/create an entry=] with <var ignore>name</var> and the
            <ins>result of [=format a value|formatting=], given |field| and the</ins>
            [=option/value=] of the <{option}> element, and append it to
            <var ignore>entry list</var>.

        1. [The steps for checkboxes, radio buttons, file uploads, and <code>_charset_</code> are
            unchanged.]

        1. Otherwise, [=entry list/create an entry=] with <var ignore>name</var> and <del>the value
            of the |field| element</del> <ins>the result of [=format a value|formatting=] |field|'s
            value, given |field|, if |field| is a [=formattable element=], or the value of |field|
            otherwise</ins>, and append it to <var ignore>entry list</var>.

        1. [If the element has a <code>dirname</code> attribute, then append its directionality
            entry. This step is unchanged.]
</div>

Note: {{FormData}}'s constructor and the {{HTMLElement/formdata}} event use the same algorithm.
Scripts calling <code>new FormData(form)</code> therefore see the formatted value, exactly as the
server does.

<h3 id=other-features>Interaction with other features</h3>

*This section is non-normative.*

: What the user sees
:: The <{input/formatter}> attribute doesn't change what the control displays or what the user
    edits.
: {{HTMLInputElement/value}}
:: The IDL attribute keeps returning the plain value. Only form submission sees the formatted value.
: Constraint validation
:: <{input/required}>, <{input/pattern}>, <code>minlength</code>, <code>maxlength</code>, and every
    other constraint check the plain value.
: <code>dirname</code>
:: The directionality entry is unchanged.
: <{select}> elements with <{select/multiple}>
:: Each selected option's value is formatted on its own, and each becomes its own entry.
: Autofill
:: Autofill fills the plain value, as it does today. The formatter applies when the form is
    submitted.


<h2 id=changes-to-html>Changes to the HTML Standard</h2>

To integrate this specification into the HTML Standard [[!HTML]], make these changes:

1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#the-input-element">the
    <code>input</code> element</a>:
    * Add <{input/formatter}> to the element's content attributes.
    * Add <code>HTMLInputElement includes FormatterAttribute;</code> to the DOM interface.
    * Add a <{input/formatter}> row to the table that summarizes which content attributes apply to
        each <{input/type}> state. Mark it "Yes" for every state whose [=value mode=] is
        [=value mode/value=], and not applicable for every other state.
1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#the-select-element">the
    <code>select</code> element</a>, add <{select/formatter}> to the element's content attributes,
    and <code>HTMLSelectElement includes FormatterAttribute;</code> to the DOM interface.
1. Add the definitions in [[#the-formatter-attribute]] as a new subsection of "Attributes common to
    form controls".
1. Apply the change in [[#patch-construct-the-entry-list]].
1. In the index of element attributes, add a row: attribute <code>formatter</code>; elements
    <code>input</code>, <code>select</code>; description "Template for the submitted value"; value
    "Text".


<h2 id=a11y>Accessibility considerations</h2>

The formatted value is never shown to the user. If the format matters to the user, for example
because they will see it later in a confirmation email, authors should describe it in the control's
label or nearby text.

Because the user no longer types the fixed characters, fields can use simpler input modes, such as
<code>inputmode="numeric"</code>, and simpler instructions. This helps users of on-screen keyboards,
voice input, and switch access.


<h2 id=security-privacy>Security and privacy considerations</h2>

* This specification exposes no new information. The formatted value contains only the control's
    value and text the author wrote.
* The <{input/formatter}> attribute is plain text substitution. It can't run script, fetch
    resources, or evaluate expressions.
* Servers must not rely on the formatter for security. User agents without support, and clients
    that aren't browsers, submit whatever values they like.


<h2 id=more-examples>More examples</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class=example id=example-country-code>
    A formatter on a <{select}> element adds the <code>+</code> to a country calling code, so the
    option values stay plain numbers.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <select name="country" formatter="+{value}">
      <option value="1">United States (+1)</option>
      <option value="44">United Kingdom (+44)</option>
    </select>
    </xmp>

    Choosing United Kingdom submits <code>country=+44</code>.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-amount>
    The user types an amount, and the server receives it with its currency.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <label>Amount (US dollars)
      <input type="number" name="amount" min="1" step="0.01" formatter="{value} USD">
    </label>
    </xmp>

    Typing <kbd>25.50</kbd> submits <code>amount=25.50 USD</code>. The <code>min</code> and
    <code>step</code> attributes check the number the user typed.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-tags>
    With <{select/multiple}>, each selected value is formatted separately.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <select name="tags" multiple formatter="tag:{value}">
      <option>news</option>
      <option>sports</option>
      <option>weather</option>
    </select>
    </xmp>

    Selecting "news" and "weather" submits <code>tags=tag:news&amp;tags=tag:weather</code>.
</div>


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<h3 id=alt-collector-element>The <code>collector</code> element</h3>

<a href="https://arronei.github.io/HTML-Additions/collector-element/spec/">The Collector Element</a>
proposes a <code>collector</code> element whose <code>pattern</code> attribute uses the same
template syntax to combine several controls into one submitted value. A collector around a single
control can do the same job as a formatter:

<xmp highlight=html>
<collector name="areaCode" pattern="({digits})">
  <input type="tel" name="digits" maxlength="3">
</collector>
</xmp>

The <{input/formatter}> attribute is lighter for the common case of one control, needs no extra
element, and keeps the control's own name. The two could be combined, for example by letting a formatted control inside
a collector contribute its formatted value.

<h3 id=alt-script>Script</h3>

Authors can already rewrite values in a {{HTMLElement/formdata}} event listener. That works for
<code>new FormData(form)</code> and for navigation, but it has to be written for each form, and it
doesn't run when script is turned off.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

Issue: <strong>Attribute name.</strong> Alternatives include <code>format</code>,
<code>submitformat</code>, and <code>valueformat</code>.

Issue: <strong>Empty values.</strong> Empty values are never formatted. Should authors be able to
format them too, for example to send a default?

Issue: <strong>Transformations.</strong> Should the template support simple transformations, such as
<code>{value:upper}</code>, trimming, or padding with zeros to a fixed length?

Issue: <strong>Reading the formatted value.</strong> Should there be a read-only
<code>formattedValue</code> IDL attribute, so scripts can show the user what will be submitted
without building a {{FormData}} object?

Issue: <strong>More controls.</strong> Should <{textarea}> elements, and <{input}> elements in the
Hidden state, accept <{input/formatter}>? Should form-associated custom elements be able to opt in?
